course: english
lesson: 60
title: Olympiad: tricky comprehension
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Tricky comprehension questions test whether you read exactly what the text says, not what you expect it to say.
explain: Watch the little words: "not untrue" means true, so "It is not untrue that Sam did not go" means Sam did not go; and "but" can join two facts that both still count.
explain: Some words point back to something earlier, and you must work out which: in "The trophy didn't fit in the suitcase because it was too big", "it" is the trophy, but with "too small", "it" is the suitcase.
explain: People do not always mean what they say: Dad's "Oh, brilliant, another rainy sports day" is sarcasm, and Tom saying he didn't eat the biscuits while brushing off crumbs is a clue that he is fibbing.
explain: Use only what the text gives you: if it says Lily rides her bike on Mondays, you cannot claim she rides it every day, and if the text does not explain why something happened, "the text does not say" can be the right answer.
fact: In "Amy is taller than Ben. Ben is taller than Cal, but Cal is taller than Dee", the order from tallest is Amy, Ben, Cal, Dee, so Dee is the shortest.
fact: "Not untrue" is a double negative meaning "true".
fact: In "The trophy didn't fit in the suitcase because it was too big", "it" means the trophy; with "too small", "it" means the suitcase.
fact: Sarcasm says the opposite of what is meant, often in a flat or annoyed voice: "Oh, brilliant" about rain means fed up.
fact: Clues in a text, like crumbs on a jumper, can show that a character is not telling the truth.
fact: "The old man the boat" is a real sentence: "the old" means old people, and "man" is a verb meaning to work or crew the boat.
fact: In "Before Mia ate breakfast, she fed the cat", feeding the cat comes first, even though breakfast is mentioned first.
fact: A "so" links a cause to its result: Ravi left his books at school, so he could not do his homework at home.
fact: A question asking what is NOT stated wants the option the text never says, such as "every day" when the text only says "on Mondays".
fact: "Dogs must be carried on the escalator" means that if you have a dog with you, you must carry it; it does not mean everyone must carry a dog.
fact: When a text does not give a reason, a good reader can say what is possible but does not claim it is certain.
exercise: "Amy is taller than Ben. Ben is taller than Cal, but Cal is taller than Dee." Who is the shortest? | Dee | Cal | Ben | Amy
exercise: In the same passage, who is the tallest? | Amy | Ben | Dee | Cal
exercise: "The trophy didn't fit in the suitcase because it was too big." What was too big? | The trophy | The suitcase | Both of them | Neither one
exercise: "The trophy didn't fit in the suitcase because it was too small." What was too small? | The suitcase | The trophy | Both of them | Neither one
exercise: "It is not untrue that Sam did not go to the party." Did Sam go? | No, he did not | Yes, he went | Maybe; we can't tell | He went twice
exercise: What does "not untrue" mean? | True | Untrue | Unknown | Half true
exercise: "'I definitely didn't eat the biscuits,' said Tom, brushing crumbs off his jumper." Is Tom telling the truth? | Probably not: the crumbs give him away | Yes, because he said "definitely" | Yes, because crumbs prove nothing | We can be sure he is honest
exercise: "'Oh, brilliant, another rainy sports day,' said Dad, staring out of the window." How does Dad feel? | Fed up | Delighted | Excited | Scared
exercise: What is Dad using when he says "Oh, brilliant"? | Sarcasm | A simile | Alliteration | A spoonerism
exercise: "The old man the boat." What does "man" mean here? | To work or crew | A grown-up male | The boat's name | Nothing; it's wrong
exercise: "Before Mia ate breakfast, she fed the cat. After breakfast, she brushed her teeth. Then she fed the fish." What did Mia do first? | She fed the cat | She ate breakfast | She brushed her teeth | She fed the fish
exercise: In the same passage, what did Mia do last? | She fed the fish | She fed the cat | She brushed her teeth | She ate breakfast
exercise: "Ravi's bag was heavy, so he left his books at school. When he got home, he couldn't do his homework." Why couldn't he? | His books were at school | His bag was too heavy to open | He was too tired after school | He had lost his only pen
exercise: "Lily has a red bike. She rides it to school on Mondays." Which of these is NOT stated? | She rides it every day | Lily has a bike | The bike is red | She rides it to school
exercise: A sign says, "Dogs must be carried on the escalator." What does it mean? | If you have a dog, carry it | Everyone has to carry a dog | Dogs must carry the people | No dogs are allowed at all
exercise: "Everyone in the class except Sam passed the test. Sam's mum was proud." Did Sam pass the test? | No | Yes | We cannot tell | Half of it
test: Jake reads "Dogs must be carried on the escalator" and panics: "But I haven't got a dog!" What did he get wrong? | The sign is only for people with dogs | The sign really says dogs are banned | The sign was about cats, not dogs | Nothing; he must go and find a dog
test: "Everyone in the class except Sam passed the test. Sam's mum was proud." Why might she be proud? | The text doesn't say; maybe he tried hard | Because Sam passed with top marks | Because everyone else failed the test | Because Sam came first in the class
test: Ella says, "'The old man the boat' isn't even a sentence!" Put her right. | It is: old people crew the boat | She's right: it has no verb at all | It's about one old man, so it's fine | It only works with "is" after "man"
test: "The ball broke the window because it was weak." What was weak? | The window | The ball | Both of them | Neither one
test: Your mate says, "'Not untrue' means false, innit, two nots." Put him right. | The two negatives cancel: it means true | He's right: it means false | It means half true | It means nobody knows
test: Liam in Leeds, dry: "Champion, me bike's got a puncture again." ("Champion" means great in Yorkshire.) How does he really feel? | Annoyed | Pleased | Proud | Calm
test: "Zoe is older than Max. Max is older than Ivy. Ivy is older than Ben." Who is the second youngest? | Ivy | Max | Ben | Zoe
test: A cartoon says "Before Mia ate breakfast, she fed the cat" means breakfast came first. Fix it. | Feeding the cat came first | Breakfast really came first | Both happened at once | The text doesn't say which
test: Bethany, Gen Z: "Lily rides her bike every day, it's literally in the text, I'm dead." ("I'm dead" means I'm laughing so hard.) What is right? | The text only says Mondays | She is right: it says every day | The text says she walks to school | The text only says weekends
test: "Tom said, 'I never touched the cake,' with icing on his nose." Which word best describes Tom's claim? | Unlikely | Proven | Certain | Polite
test: "Nobody in the team is not tired." Is anyone in the team tired? | Yes: everyone is tired | No: nobody is tired | Only some of them are | The text doesn't say
exam: It is given that Amy exceeds Ben in height, Ben exceeds Cal, and Cal exceeds Dee. Identify the individual of least height. | Dee | Cal | Ben | Amy
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the assertion "It is not untrue that Sam did not go to the party." | Sam did not attend the party | Sam attended the party | Sam's attendance is unknown | Sam attended only briefly
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the notice "Dogs must be carried on the escalator." | Persons with dogs must carry them | All persons must carry a dog | Dogs are prohibited on the escalator | Dogs may carry small items
exam: Identify which of the following is not stated in the passage "Lily has a red bike. She rides it to school on Mondays." | Lily rides it daily | Lily owns a bike | The bike is red | She rides to school
